One way to think about the assignment is as follows. Field_1 has the highest bird‐threat level (0.25) and requires 4 drones; it already had 4 drones assigned, so we keep Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_5, and Drone_7 protecting Field_1. Field_4 has a moderate threat level (0.07) but is underprotected (2 of 3 needed). To improve protection where it matters most, we “steal” one drone from a low-threat field. Field_3, with a threat level of only 0.03, was fully protected by 2 drones. Reassigning one drone (here, Drone_8, which is slightly closer to Field_4 by location consideration) from Field_3 to Field_4 increases that field’s protection to the full complement of 3 drones. Drone_2 remains with Field_3 (making it partially protected, which is acceptable given its very low threat level) and Field_2 continues unprotected (threat 0.03) as our limited drone resources are best focused on the more at‐risk fields. This distribution minimizes potential damage by ensuring that the fields with higher threat levels (Field_1 and Field_4) receive full protection.
